A messy first ascension but an ascension nonetheless. Nice. You did a
lot of stuff right.

1. You used your magic markers to get your AC down south ASAP.
Getting early game AC is really, really important. You gave it
priority, and this is good. You also wished for SDSM early on. This
was also good.
2. You saved wishes for an emergency.
So you didn't bite the dust versus the Big D or archons.
3. You kept a stash to avoid burdening yourself.
It's much easier to keep a stash at Sokoban as a wizard due to the
Eye's branchporting ability. Still, having an altar close by is a Very
Good Thing.
4. Used your pet to get spellbooks from shops.
Reading every spellbook you can is good strategy for a wizard.
5. You genocided L and h.
This is almost a no-brainer in vanilla, but hey, it still counts in
your favor.
6. You killed powerful demons quickly, especially Orcus, and exercised
caution when fighting powerful foes.
7. You maximized your luck ASAP.


However, you could have made your life much, *much* easier.

1. You waited until the castle to write charging.
Wizards can write *any scroll or book* once luck is maximized. Extra
marker charges are a Good Thing.

2. You didn't learn Finger of Death.
You received a spellbook of Finger of Death upon crowning. It can be a
lifesaver sometimes (read: plane of air).

3. You genocided useful monsters.
D is for Dragon. R is for disenchanter. Dragons have a harmless attack
once you have reflection. Black dragons can save your ass if you have
conflict. Dragon scales are... well, you know. And don't get me
started about genociding all those tasty giants! Minotaurs are
powerful allies if you have charming spells, and titans, the real
nasties, *cannot be genocided*.

And BTW, R are a nice buffer against nasty hitty things in summoning
swarms. And they're harmless if you've got a robe or CoMR (any MC3
cloak, including Elven, in fact).
4. Wishes could have been spent more wisely.
A robe is almost never worth a wish. Kill a priest with your attack
spells and take *his* robe if you're chaotic. And sometimes (check
with a wand of probing), priests have cloaks of protection/magic
resistance instead. If you're a valk, and that priest has a cloak of
magic resistance, you will want it.
As a wizard, a helm of brilliance is a greater aide to spellcasting.

Oh well, I did worse in my own wiz YAFAP, wished for gauntlets of
power and a cornuthaum.
5. Poor food management.
When nethack gives you a ton of something, it's for a reason. Orcish
helms help avoid falling rock traps in early game, daggers can save
your life time and again, etc. And nethack gives you a ****ton of K-
rations, the most nutritious one-turn comestible in the game, at the
castle. Take them. Take ten. Take twenty if you have a bag of holding.
They are lifesavers during the ascension run; only royal jelly is
better. Then again, if you had some tasty *dragons* or *giants*
around, food wouldn't have been such a problem, eh? ;)

Once again, well done.


